 the unenrolled possibility  right you want to even if you know that  you voted before you've been registered  to vote  there are systems in different states  that will unenroll you based on certain  characteristics I believe that it was in  Indiana where we covered the cross-check  system which and I don't remember for  sure and I'm going off the top of my  head if this provision was enacted or  proposed but there was at least an idea  to unenroll people who haven't voted in  two consecutive elections or something  like that doesn't even matter what the  specifics are even if you voted maybe  you didn't vote in November of 2016 you  thought Hillary was going to run away  with it whatever and now you want to  vote make sure you're still registered  still use the link in the youtube video  description or go to usa.gov forward  slash forward slash registered to vote  with hyphens between the three words the  other thing is be aware of voter ID  requirements different states have  different rules for what ID you need and  you want to have this information  present when you go to vote in November  number one because if you need ID you  want to have the right ID and show up  with it to not have any problems voting  but number two you want to know if you  don't need certain documents because  then if either you are asked for  documents you don't actually need you  can report it if others are asked for  documents that they don't really need  and maybe they don't know you can try to  intercede in that to make sure that  people aren't turned away due to  fabricated or imaginary voter ID  requirements and what we are also  linking to is a website where you can  view that information it is a link to  NCSL org they have a specific if you  google NCSL voter ID it will take you to  a page that will in very explicit terms  tell you everything that you need to  vote in certain states and it will make  it clear when you don't need other  documents to vote in those states now  let me go even further okay if you have  registered if you have voted before so  you know that you were registered and  you look it up and you are still  registered to vote that's great and then  you confirm that there have been no new  voter ID requirements  in your state and you know exactly what  it is that you need to bring there's  actually still more that you can do and  you can in many places register to be a  sort of election observer and it's a  volunteer thing but it is really really  important and it would be great if this  wasn't a partisan thing but we know that  when voter turnout is higher the more  progressive candidate tends to win  adjusting for money right so like the  candidate with more money really tends  to win most of the time but when you  adjust for money the more progressive  candidate does better when more people  vote within a Republican primary that's  also even sometimes true where if you've  got sort of more centrist Republican and  an extreme Republican when voter turnout  goes way way up the more moderate  Republican tends to win in cross party  elections Democrats and Republicans  Democrats generally do way better when  more people vote so even just showing up  and voting is political we know it helps  the left when more people vote become an  election observer so that you can be  there in November of 2018 and make sure  that people are not turned away either  with bogus ID demands or whatever else  the case may be really important stuff  and and I also want to mention if you  have any questions about a specific rule  google it this stuff is widely available  online you can even tweet me and I have  a bunch of different resources that are  abundantly clear about what the rules  are as far as ID registration and  whatever else I'm really thrilled to  tell you that today's program is made  possible in part by better help calm  slash Pakman as many of you know I have  been working very hard on this program  to reduce stigma around mental he